    "narrative": "For LawDog today, we are leaning heavily into favorable matchups against middle-of-the-road arms to chase efficiency, with **Taylor Ward** (71.19) and **Dylan Beavers** (69.52) anchoring our outfield as the two strongest plays. Both benefit from a plus-park environment and a exploitable matchup against Littell, whose 4.82 xERA makes him the primary target for our offense today. Similarly, **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) provides a high-floor utility play, benefiting from a +12% dynamic platoon edge and a strong xwOBA projection of 0.374, allowing him to outperform several regular starters despite the neutral park factors. **Daulton Varsho** (66.20) rounds out a productive core, specifically bolstered by his +10% platoon edge and the fact that Rocker\u2019s delivery profile offers a distinct advantage for stolen base opportunities, which we continue to prioritize to remain competitive in our most scarce category.\n\nThe toughest decision on the bench involved **Luis Arraez**, whose 41.85 score keeps him well within the \"Marginal\" start tier but ultimately fell short of our high-efficiency threshold. While his contact profile is stable, the matchup against Sale (3.01 xERA) is a significant drag on his ceiling, creating a -19% penalty due to the elite opposing arm. We have opted to prioritize the platoon and park-inflated scores of players like **Tyler Freeman** (63.25) and **Lawrence Butler** (60.97) over the high-floor, low-ceiling profile **Luis Arraez** offers against a top-tier pitcher. Elsewhere, we continue to monitor the status of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who remains sidelined on the IL, while **Jung Hoo Lee** (35.77) stays firmly on the bench, failing to clear our 40-point floor due to the brutal matchup against the left-handed Sale.\n\nRisks remain present in the infield, particularly with **Matt Chapman** (42.80) and **Bryson Stott** (46.26), who both face elite or difficult pitching dynamics today. While **Matt Chapman** enjoys a +10% boost for hitting at the top of the order and a favorable wind, the -19% SP skill penalty against Sale makes him a volatile asset. Owners should track the lineups for **Yainer Diaz** and **Cal Raleigh** closely; while both are projected as mid-range starts, any late-scratch or order drop could easily push them toward the sub-40 danger zone. We are positioned well to capitalize on the weak pitching staff facing our outfielders, provided we maintain our discipline on game caps.",
